WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.667
Question 0002: swapping the values of two variables,
a student-level question.

2
00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:10.167
Three parts, and each part adds a constraint:
with a temp variable, without one, and bits only.

3
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:15.867
The function swap takes two variables, a and b,
and swaps them in place.

4
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:20.600
At the end, a holds the original value of b,
and b holds the original value of a.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.933
Part A: the simplest way, with a temp variable.

6
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:29.167
We have two variables: a equals 5, and b equals 3.

7
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:32.667
The goal: a should hold 3, and b should hold 5.

8
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:37.767
The idea: an extra variable, temp,
that saves the value of a before it is overwritten.

9
00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:41.967
Step 1: temp = a. The value 5 is saved in temp.

10
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:47.000
Step 2: a = b. Now a holds 3,
and the original 5 is still saved in temp.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.800
Step 3: b = temp, and b gets 5.
The swap is complete.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.267
The same example in the page's box:
the code, and under it the value of every variable.

13
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:01.233
temp keeps a0, the value 5,
before v[i] is overwritten.

14
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:06.200
v[i] gets b0, and the symbolic column
shows a0 is kept only in temp.

15
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.100
v[j] gets a0 from temp.
At the end a = b0 and b = a0.

16
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.567
Now let's write it in Python, line by line.

17
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:20.567
Python cannot pass an int by reference,
so we pass a list v and two indices, i and j.

18
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:24.867
Here v[i] plays the role of a,
and v[j] plays the role of b.

19
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:30.467
Line 2: save v[i], the value of a, in temp,
before it is overwritten.

20
00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:36.067
Line 3: now v[i] may be overwritten,
and it gets the value of b.

21
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.600
Line 4: v[j] gets the original value of a,
which is saved in temp.

22
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.700
On the right, the page's box runs the same code
on [5, 3], with i = 0 and j = 1.

23
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.833
First temp gets v[i], that is 5.

24
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:55.967
Then v[i] gets v[j], that is 3.

25
00:01:55.967 --> 00:02:00.133
Finally v[j] gets temp,
and the list is now [3, 5].

26
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:06.167
Why it works: let's follow 5 and 3,
and see where they are after each step.

27
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:11.567
In every row, each of them is in at least one variable.
No value is ever lost.

28
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:17.867
The cost: three assignments, so O(1) time,
and extra memory of one int variable.

29
00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:22.167
The trap: writing a = b before saving a.

30
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:26.167
The value 5 is erased.
Now both a and b hold 3.

31
00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:30.967
The assignment b = a no longer helps:
both variables end up with 3.

32
00:02:30.967 --> 00:02:35.067
So saving into temp must come
before the first overwrite.

33
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:41.067
Part B: the same swap, with no temp variable at all.
Arithmetic operations are allowed.

34
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:46.867
There is no temp. So the first value we write
must keep information about both values.

35
00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:50.467
The idea: the sum, 8, holds both values together.

36
00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:54.700
Knowing the sum and one of the values,
a subtraction gives back the other.

37
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.200
Step 1: a = a + b, and now a holds 8,
the sum of both.

38
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.100
Step 2: b = a − b, that is, 8 minus 3,
and b gets 5.

39
00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:09.067
Step 3: a = a − b, that is, 8 minus 5,
and a gets 3. The values are swapped.

40
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:15.267
In the page's box, the same three lines,
and a symbolic column that follows a0 and b0.

41
00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:18.933
v[i] gets the sum, 8,
symbolically a0 + b0.

42
00:03:18.933 --> 00:03:23.400
v[j] gets the sum minus b0,
that is 8 − 3 = 5, which is a0.

43
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:27.867
v[i] gets the sum minus a0,
that is 8 − 5 = 3, which is b0.

44
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:32.400
The page's code: the same three lines, on v[i] and v[j].

45
00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:38.533
The same signature as in Part A:
a list v and two indices, with no temp.

46
00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:45.633
Line 2: v[i] is overwritten with the sum of both,
and the comment notes a = a0 + b0.

47
00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:53.533
Line 3: the sum minus b0 leaves a0,
and that is the new value of v[j].

48
00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:01.367
Line 4: the sum minus a0 leaves b0,
and that is the new value of v[i].

49
00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:06.233
And we run it beside the page's box,
on the same example, v = [5, 3].

50
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:10.367
First v[i] gets the sum, 8.

51
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:14.500
Then v[j] gets 8 minus 3, that is 5.

52
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.633
Finally v[i] gets 8 minus 5, that is 3.

53
00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:22.633
In Python the sum never overflows,
because Python integers are unbounded.

54
00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:28.400
Why it works: on the number line,
going back 3 steps from 8 lands on 5.

55
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.500
And going back 5 steps from 8 lands on 3.
No information is lost.

56
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.333
And in general, with a₀ and b₀:
at the end a = b₀ and b = a₀, for any two values.

57
00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:44.633
What about overflow? In SystemVerilog, int is 32 bits,
and the arithmetic is modulo 2³².

58
00:04:44.633 --> 00:04:48.733
The values sit on a wheel:
right after 2³¹ − 1 comes −2³¹.

59
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:53.533
Say a = 2³¹ − 1 and b = 1.
The sum crosses the seam and wraps around to −2³¹.

60
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:58.033
Step 2: the subtraction turns the wheel back,
and b gets 2³¹ − 1.

61
00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:02.533
Step 3: a gets 1.
The swap is correct, even though the sum overflowed.

62
00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:07.133
All the equalities also hold modulo 2³²,
so the overflow does no harm.

63
00:05:07.933 --> 00:05:13.033
The cost: O(1) time and zero extra memory,
which is the whole point of this part.

64
00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:18.800
The trap: calling swap with the same variable twice,
for example swap(x, x).

65
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.100
Here a and b are two names for the same variable.
Step 1: x gets 5 + 5, that is, 10.

66
00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.067
Step 2: x gets x − x, that is, 0.
The original value is lost.

67
00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:31.667
Step 3 leaves 0.
The temp version is not affected by this.

68
00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:36.467
In Python, the function is guarded by a check:
if i == j, return at once.

69
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:41.800
Part C: no temp variable and no arithmetic.
Only &, |, ^ and ~ are allowed.

70
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:47.600
Let's write both values in bits:
5 is 0101, and 3 is 0011.

71
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.267
The idea: XOR, which gives 1 exactly when the bits differ.

72
00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:56.767
So x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x.
XOR is thus both the addition and the subtraction of part B.

73
00:05:57.767 --> 00:06:02.500
Step 1: a = a ^ b. A bit of a flips
in every column where b has a 1.

74
00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:07.733
Now a is 0110, that is, 6:
it marks the columns where 5 and 3 differ.

75
00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:13.400
Step 2: b = a ^ b, and b flips where a is 1.
The result is 0101, that is, 5.

76
00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.967
Step 3: a = a ^ b, and a flips where b is 1.
The result is 0011, that is, 3.

77
00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:24.000
And the page's box, this time with XOR:
the same three lines, with ^.

78
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.900
v[i] gets 5 ^ 3, that is 6,
symbolically a0 ^ b0.

79
00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.933
v[j] gets 6 ^ 3, that is 5:
b0 cancels out and a0 is left.

80
00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:35.967
v[i] gets 6 ^ 5, that is 3:
a0 cancels out and b0 is left.

81
00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:40.800
The code from the page: the same three lines,
with ^ instead of + and −.

82
00:06:42.333 --> 00:06:45.733
Again the same signature: a list v and two indices.

83
00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:52.767
Line 2: v[i] gets a0 ^ b0,
the columns where the two values differ.

84
00:06:55.967 --> 00:07:00.467
Line 3: an XOR with b0 cancels it,
leaving a0, the new value of v[j].

85
00:07:03.633 --> 00:07:08.133
Line 4: an XOR with a0 cancels it,
leaving b0, the new value of v[i].

86
00:07:08.833 --> 00:07:12.300
And now the run on 5 and 3,
beside the page's box.

87
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.433
First v[i] gets 5 ^ 3, that is 6.

88
00:07:16.433 --> 00:07:20.567
Then v[j] gets 6 ^ 3, that is 5.

89
00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:24.700
Finally v[i] gets 6 ^ 5, that is 3.

90
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:31.000
Why it works: XOR acts on each column alone,
with no carry, so checking one bit is enough.

91
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:36.100
There are only four combinations of a₀ and b₀.
We run the three steps on each one.

92
00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:40.267
In all four, step 2 gives back a₀,
and step 3 gives back b₀.

93
00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:43.767
And in algebra: b₀ ^ b₀ cancels to 0, leaving a₀.

94
00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:48.967
The cost: O(1) time and zero memory,
with no carry and no overflow.

95
00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:54.433
The three steps depend on each other, so they run serially.
It is a trick, not an optimization.

96
00:07:55.233 --> 00:08:00.833
First trap: the same variable again.
Since x ^ x is 0, step 1 already erases the value.

97
00:08:01.433 --> 00:08:05.467
Second trap: why not & or |?
Because they lose information.

98
00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:10.500
Where b is 0, a & b comes out 0
whether a was 0 or 1.

99
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:15.100
The same with | when b is 1.
After that, a can no longer be recovered.

100
00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.700
Only with XOR, when b is known,
can a always be recovered.

101
00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.900
To sum up: a temp variable, a sum and subtractions,
or three XOR operations.

102
00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:28.133
Try all three versions in the box
on the page of question 0002 on the site.
